Part 1 — Introduction

On screen: Open your Chapter 7 project in your IDE. Display LicenseService.java.
What to say:

Video narration — Introduction
In this demonstration, I will be exploring resilience patterns in Spring Microservices.
In a microservices architecture, individual services communicate with one another to perform different operations. However, these services can experience failures, slow responses, or periods of unavailability.
If one service becomes unavailable, it can affect other services that depend on it.
To help prevent these problems, Spring provides integration with Resilience4j, which allows us to implement different resilience patterns.
In this demonstration, I will focus on four important patterns: Circuit Breaker, Rate Limiter, Retry, and Bulkhead.
I will also demonstrate how a fallback method provides an alternative response when an operation fails.

Part 2 — Explain the four annotations
On screen: Scroll to getLicensesByOrganization() in LicenseService.java.

Point to each annotation as you explain it.
Video narration — Four resilience patterns
Here we can see the getLicensesByOrganization method in our LicenseService class.
This method retrieves licensing information for a particular organization.
Above the method, we have four resilience annotations.
The first is the Circuit Breaker annotation. Its purpose is to monitor failures and prevent repeated calls to a service that is experiencing problems.
The second is the Rate Limiter. This controls how many requests can be processed within a specified period.
The third is Retry. This allows the application to attempt an operation again when certain failures occur.
Finally, we have Bulkhead. This limits concurrent operations to prevent one part of the application from consuming too many resources.
Notice that these annotations reference configuration names. This allows us to configure their behaviour separately in our bootstrap.yml file.

Part 3 — Explain the YAML configuration

On screen: Open:

licensing-service/src/main/resources/bootstrap.yml
Find each configuration block.
However, because your current annotation uses Type.SEMAPHORE, those thread-pool settings 
are not the active bulkhead configuration for this method.
What to say:
Video narration — YAML configuration
Now I am opening the bootstrap.yml configuration file.
This file allows us to configure how each resilience pattern behaves without placing all
the configuration directly inside our Java code.
For example, our Circuit Breaker uses a failure rate threshold of 50 percent.
Our Rate Limiter allows five requests within a five-second period.
Our Retry configuration allows up to five attempts, with a ten-second delay between attempts.
Finally, our Bulkhead configuration limits the number of concurrent operations.
This separation between Java annotations and YAML configuration makes the application 
easier to maintain and configure.

Part 4 — Explain and demonstrate the fallback
On screen: Return to LicenseService.java and find buildFallbackLicenseList().

What to say:
Video narration — Fallback method
Next, I want to demonstrate the fallback method.
A fallback method provides an alternative response when an operation fails.
In this example, if the Licensing Service encounters a configured failure,
the fallback method creates a placeholder License object.
Instead of simply exposing an exception to the client, the application 
returns a message explaining that licensing information is currently unavailable.
This helps provide a more controlled response when something goes wrong.

